WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.155
OT recovery requires more than server backups.

00:00:03.155 --> 00:00:07.083
Preserve controller logic, safety and control configurations, HMI applications,

00:00:07.083 --> 00:00:11.011
engineering projects, network and security-device settings, firmware references, licenses,

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:15.811
historian definitions, recipes, and the data required by the recovery objective.

00:00:15.811 --> 00:00:20.612
System images can accelerate recovery for HMIs, engineering workstations, and servers,

00:00:20.612 --> 00:00:24.976
but hardware, drivers, vendor software, and activation requirements must match.

00:00:24.976 --> 00:00:28.904
Record the operating system, application version, patches, device interfaces,

00:00:28.904 --> 00:00:30.214
and restoration sequence.

00:00:30.214 --> 00:00:35.014
Keep protected copies offline or otherwise separated from ordinary administrative access.

00:00:35.014 --> 00:00:39.815
Use access control, integrity checks, encryption where appropriate, and multiple locations.

00:00:39.815 --> 00:00:45.925
Ransomware resilience depends on backups that the compromised environment cannot easily alter or delete.

00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:49.853
Restore testing is the proof that a backup works.

00:00:49.853 --> 00:00:52.908
Use a lab, spare device, test environment,

00:00:52.908 --> 00:00:56.836
or vendor-supported process where production restoration would be unsafe.

00:00:56.836 --> 00:01:02.073
Verify not only that files open, but that logic, communications, alarms, licenses,

00:01:02.073 --> 00:01:04.255
and dependent services function together.

00:01:04.255 --> 00:01:06.437
Vendor-specific requirements can determine success.

00:01:06.437 --> 00:01:10.801
Some systems require exact firmware, proprietary cables, hardware keys, certificates,

00:01:10.801 --> 00:01:12.111
or approved procedures.

00:01:12.111 --> 00:01:15.602
Maintain recovery kits, compatible spares, software media, credentials,

00:01:15.602 --> 00:01:17.348
and current support contacts.

00:01:17.348 --> 00:01:22.585
Track backup age, scope, owner, storage location, protection, and last restore test.

00:01:22.585 --> 00:01:26.949
After every approved change, confirm that recoverable copies were updated.

00:01:26.949 --> 00:01:32.186
A backup inventory should map directly to critical processes and recovery sequences,

00:01:32.186 --> 00:01:37.860
ensuring the organization can restore a known-good operational state rather than isolated files.
